item.text:
{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [
        "Exact-subject claims are relied on for promotion, closure or release.",
        "Finite evidence may observe a weaker or different subject or basis.",
        "Constructors may be probabilistic or otherwise fallible.",
        "Each reliance boundary has a decision owner."
      ],
      "definition": "F6, claims, evidence, assessment and acceptance, covers EVALUATION §§1–5. Its instance premises describe governed work, are not commitments and do not follow from finite capacity alone. Its foundation table traces consequences, rule owners, added choices and consumers; the table itself decides nothing.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":75,
          "quote":"They are conditions of the work\nthis family governs, not commitments, and do not follow from finite\ncapacity alone.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F1.%20Claims%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20claims%2C%20evidence%2C%20assessment%20and%20acceptance",
          "start_line":73
        }
      ],
      "key":"claims_assurance_family",
      "label":"Claims, evidence, assessment and acceptance"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["The claim bears on promotion, closure or release."],
      "definition":"A load-bearing claim identifies its exact subject, claimed property, governing basis, witness-to-claim relation, nearest excluded weaker property and falsifier before witness selection.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":82,
          "quote":"a load-bearing claim names its exact subject, the property claimed, the governing basis, the relation between witness and claim, the nearest weaker property it excludes and its falsifier before a witness is selected",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F1.%20Claims%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20claims%2C%20evidence%2C%20assessment%20and%20acceptance",
          "start_line":82
        }
      ],
      "key":"load_bearing_claim",
      "label":"Load-bearing claim"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The constitutional and accepted-design relations defining what a claim means.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":135,
          "quote":"the semantic basis, the constitutional\nand accepted-design relations that define what a claim means",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F2.%20Separation",
          "start_line":134
        }
      ],
      "key":"semantic_basis",
      "label":"Semantic basis"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The exact artifacts, events, logs, tests and observations used to evaluate a claim.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":137,
          "quote":"the evidence\nbasis, the exact artifacts, events, logs, tests and observations used to\nevaluate it",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F2.%20Separation",
          "start_line":135
        }
      ],
      "key":"evidence_basis",
      "label":"Evidence basis"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A current view derived from admitted semantic and evidence bases.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":138,
          "quote":"the state projection, a current view derived from admitted\nsemantic and evidence bases.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F2.%20Separation",
          "start_line":137
        }
      ],
      "key":"state_projection",
      "label":"State projection"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Structural conformance decides closed syntax, types, identities, references, bases and mechanically decidable predicates; semantic fidelity decides meaning and materiality. These are separate claims.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":163,
          "quote":"Structural conformance decides closed syntax, types, identities, references,\nbases and mechanically decidable predicates. Semantic fidelity decides\nmeaning and materiality.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F2.%20Separation",
          "start_line":161
        }
      ],
      "key":"structural_and_semantic_assessment",
      "label":"Structural conformance and semantic fidelity"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["Applicable promotion boundaries require independence under TICKETS policy or an additional governing-basis requirement."],
      "definition":"Assessment separate from construction, evaluating the exact live subject, authority paths, proof and installed state. Constructor self-review remains construction output. EVALUATION defines independence; TICKETS owns its required trigger boundaries and the governing basis may add boundaries.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":183,
          "quote":"A constructor's self-review is construction output, not assessment. Where\npromotion needs independent assessment, the assessor evaluates the exact\nlive subject, authority paths, proof and installed state, not the\nconstructor's summary",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F3.%20Independent%20assessment",
          "start_line":180
        }
      ],
      "key":"independent_assessment",
      "label":"Independent assessment"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Author identity identifies the party responsible for a realised subject's content. Actor identity identifies whoever performs an operation and is not itself authority. Independence depends on authorship and evidence conditions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":224,
          "quote":"Author identity is the party responsible for the content of a realised\nsubject. It is distinct from actor identity, whoever performs one\noperation, which is an identity and not an authority",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F3.%20Independent%20assessment",
          "start_line":217
        }
      ],
      "key":"author_and_actor_identity",
      "label":"Author identity and actor identity"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A review returns one core result per declared predicate actually evaluated, together with exact subject and basis, consulted evidence, consequence-ranked findings, evidence and smallest bounded correction for each finding, residual uncertainty and invalidation conditions.",
      "exceptions": ["A planned predicate not evaluated has no result."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":250,
          "quote":"A review returns one core result\n([FOUNDATIONS §4, Results](FOUNDATIONS.md#results)) for each declared\npredicate it evaluated, with: the exact subject and basis, the evidence\nconsulted, the findings ranked by consequence, each finding's evidence and\nthe smallest bounded correction, the residual uncertainty, and the\ninvalidation conditions.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F4.%20Review%20results",
          "start_line":244
        }
      ],
      "key":"review_result",
      "label":"Review result"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["A review returns findings with technical triage."],
      "definition":"Technical triage is the assessor's evidence-bound assessment against the exact evaluated claim and basis. Severity grades consequence as evaluation evidence. Priority, current promotion-boundary effect and disposition belong to the decision owner. Triage payload classes complete, indeterminate and not_applicable are distinct from core results, dispositions, priorities and lifecycle states.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":267,
          "quote":"Where a review returns findings with technical triage, the triage is the\nassessor's evidence-bound assessment of each finding against the exact\nevaluated claim and basis. Severity, the assessor's grade of consequence,\nis evaluation evidence. Priority, the effect on the current promotion\nboundary and the disposition are the decision owner's",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F4.%20Review%20results%2FResult%20and%20triage%20projection",
          "start_line":263
        }
      ],
      "key":"technical_triage",
      "label":"Technical triage"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["The predicate has actually been evaluated."],
      "definition":"The review projection distinguishes satisfied, falsified, indeterminate, out_of_frame and invalid_basis. Missing evaluation is absence of a result, not an additional value or an indeterminate value.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":283,
          "quote":"A predicate\nnot performed has no result and so no triage payload; the payload class\n`indeterminate` never stands for it.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F4.%20Review%20results%2FResult%20and%20triage%20projection",
          "start_line":279
        },
        {
          "end_line":291,
          "quote":"| invalid_basis | a basis failure; triage `not_applicable`; invalidation evidence and basis-repair pressure | refuse to consume the result and return through the declared basis re-entry relation |",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F4.%20Review%20results%2FResult%20and%20triage%20projection",
          "start_line":291
        }
      ],
      "key":"core_review_results",
      "label":"Core review-result domain"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Acceptance is the decision owner's disposition over an unchanged evaluated subject, bounded to its named instance and interval. Work-item closure is satisfaction of that item's stated closing condition.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":319,
          "quote":"A work item closes by the condition it stated, not by a reviewer's approval;\na reviewer result is an input to closure, never the closure. Green labels,\ndocument counts, review counts and historical outcomes are not acceptance.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F5.%20Acceptance",
          "start_line":317
        }
      ],
      "key":"acceptance",
      "label":"Acceptance and work-item closure"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["The accepted subject is a successor."],
      "definition":"Acceptance evidence identifying the exact predecessor immutable cut and one semantic disposition for every predecessor claim capable of affecting the successor; RELEASE §9 owns its disposition values and evaluation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":336,
          "quote":"Where the subject is a successor, its acceptance evidence includes the\nsuccessor disposition record of [RELEASE §9](RELEASE.md#9-successor-baseline-conservation-stdo-up-015)\n(STDO-UP-015), which states the law: the exact predecessor immutable cut\nthe successor evolves, and one semantic disposition for every predecessor\nclaim capable of affecting the successor.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F5.%20Acceptance",
          "start_line":330
        }
      ],
      "key":"successor_disposition_record",
      "label":"Successor disposition record"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A finding is a defect reported against the exact evaluated claim. Blocking current promotion is the decision owner's act over a finding. EVALUATION §5 owns the common blocking rule; profiles and work carriers add only domain triggers.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":346,
          "quote":"A finding is a defect a review reports against the exact claim it\nevaluated (§4). Blocking current promotion is the decision owner's act\nover that finding (§4, Result and triage projection). This section is the\none statement of when a finding can block; profiles and work carriers\nrefer to it and add only their domain triggers.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F5.%20Acceptance%2FWhen%20a%20finding%20blocks%20promotion",
          "start_line":342
        }
      ],
      "key":"promotion_finding",
      "label":"Finding and promotion block"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"A realisation conforms to specification Σ when its interpreted behaviour satisfies Σ under a declared interpretation contract and independent judgment. Semantic conformance uses the governing semantic basis as Σ.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":390,
          "quote":"A realisation aⱼ conforms to a specification Σ when its interpreted behaviour\nsatisfies Σ under a declared interpretation contract (P1) and an independent\njudgment (P6).",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F6.%20Conformance%20of%20realisations",
          "start_line":388
        }
      ],
      "key":"realisation_conformance",
      "label":"Conformance of realisations"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The equivalence ladder lists, without ordering or implication, conformance, semantic equivalence, computational equivalence and categorical equivalence. Semantic equivalence here concerns identical interpreted behaviour of two realisations, or a realisation and declared reference outcome, on a stated domain including behaviour outside Σ; it is not bounded-context concept equivalence. Computational equivalence concerns the same function at equal cost for declared workloads on architecture H. Categorical equivalence concerns categories and maps; preservation of identities and composition is a further distinct claim.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":404,
          "quote":"the rungs are listed, not ordered, and no rung implies\nanother. Conformance, semantic equivalence, computational equivalence and\ncategorical equivalence are separate claims with separate evidence",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F6.%20Conformance%20of%20realisations",
          "start_line":396
        },
        {
          "end_line":415,
          "quote":"Computational\nequivalence claims that two realisations compute the same function at\nequal cost for declared workloads on a declared architecture H.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F6.%20Conformance%20of%20realisations",
          "start_line":408
        }
      ],
      "key":"equivalence_claims",
      "label":"Equivalence ladder"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["A specification is arranged in layers."],
      "definition":"Layered sufficiency means a competent actor can derive a conformant next layer from each layer. Derivation need not be unique. Reconstructability requires a correct route, with scope determined by the claim.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":431,
          "quote":"A specification arranged in layers is sufficient when\na competent actor can derive a conformant next layer from each layer; the\nderivation need not be unique",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F6.%20Conformance%20of%20realisations",
          "start_line":428
        }
      ],
      "key":"layered_sufficiency",
      "label":"Layered sufficiency and reconstructability"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["A task builds or assesses one successor member against its predecessor."],
      "definition":"A reconstruction return separates three claims and their evidence: semantic conservation reads both texts against a predecessor claim's disposition; demonstrated reduction traces a cheaper corresponding deciding path on a declared measure with tradeoffs; mere relocation moves text between owners or carriers without changed meaning or a traced deciding-path change.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":478,
          "quote":"Demonstrated\nreduction: a deciding path of the successor costs less than the same path\nin the predecessor on a declared measure, shown by the trace of both paths\nwith the declared tradeoffs. Mere relocation: text moved from one owner or\ncarrier to another without a change in meaning and without a traced\nchange in any deciding path.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F6.%20Conformance%20of%20realisations%2FReturn%20of%20a%20reconstruction",
          "start_line":473
        }
      ],
      "key":"reconstruction_return",
      "label":"Return of a reconstruction"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "One specification may have several realisations.",
        "Fallible constructors produce realisations, frames and methods relied on by other actors.",
        "Qualification evidence comprises finite cases run within declared actor envelopes."
      ],
      "definition":"F7, conformance, equivalence and qualification, covers EVALUATION §§6–7 and CONFORMANCE. Its instance premises are conditions of governed work, not commitments or consequences of finite capacity alone. It explicitly invokes P0's actor-change premise: a later actor may reacquire declared sources but not private memory. Its table traces consequences and added policy choices without deciding them.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":501,
          "quote":"P0's actor-change premise, that the actor of one\nstep need not be the actor of the next and may re-acquire declared sources\nbut not private memory, is cited by name.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F6.%20Conformance%20of%20realisations%2FFoundation%20to%20law%3A%20conformance%2C%20equivalence%20and%20qualification",
          "start_line":497
        }
      ],
      "key":"conformance_qualification_family",
      "label":"Conformance, equivalence and qualification"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Document section grouping frame qualification and qualification of the method; the heading alone adds no qualification procedure.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":545,
          "quote":"## 7. Qualification of frames and of the method",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F7.%20Qualification%20of%20frames%20and%20of%20the%20method",
          "start_line":545
        }
      ],
      "key":"qualification_section",
      "label":"Qualification of frames and of the method"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Qualification of a candidate frame is proportional to its claims and binds the exact evaluated frame revision, actor envelope, evaluation family, basis class and evidence conditions.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":578,
          "quote":"Qualification binds the exact frame revision, actor envelope, evaluation\nfamily, basis class and evidence conditions evaluated. It does not\ngeneralise to an unevaluated population by naming the frame generically",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F7.%20Qualification%20of%20frames%20and%20of%20the%20method%2FFrame%20qualification",
          "start_line":575
        }
      ],
      "key":"frame_qualification",
      "label":"Frame qualification"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Fit asks whether an actor class can hold a frame; context fit compares measured selected-representation size with declared capacity. Adequate attention asks whether actors actually operate the frame adequately on declared tasks and conditions. Fit and attention require separate evaluations.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":587,
          "quote":"Whether the actor class\noperates the frame adequately is adequate attention, and only actual-use\nevidence establishes it",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F7.%20Qualification%20of%20frames%20and%20of%20the%20method%2FFrame%20qualification",
          "start_line":585
        }
      ],
      "key":"fit_and_adequate_attention",
      "label":"Fit and adequate attention"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"Qualification of generic method sufficiency uses the ordinary work boundary, construction and assessment relation, and layered sufficiency, with no second process. Constructor, freeze, assessor, acceptance and release protocols are supplied by governing authority rather than defined by this member.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":608,
          "quote":"The method is qualified the way it qualifies anything else. Qualification\nof generic method sufficiency consumes the work boundary (FOUNDATIONS §2),\nthe construction and assessment relation of ACTIVATION §1 and the layered\nsufficiency of §6; it creates no second process.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F7.%20Qualification%20of%20frames%20and%20of%20the%20method%2FMethod%20self-qualification",
          "start_line":605
        }
      ],
      "key":"method_qualification",
      "label":"Method self-qualification"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"The qualification population includes every claimed function and failure relation: declare, activate, evaluate, conjoin, translate, audit_coverage, revise, result consumption and material composition. These are described source operations, not operations performed by this interpretation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":632,
          "quote":"The qualification population is every function and failure relation the\nmethod claims (REFERENCE_FRAME §5), each with its required representative\nobservations (P6):",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F7.%20Qualification%20of%20frames%20and%20of%20the%20method%2FMethod%20self-qualification",
          "start_line":630
        }
      ],
      "key":"method_function_population",
      "label":"Method-function qualification population"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["Guidance derived from the method changes."],
      "definition":"Compressions, frame packets, skills and other method projections have separately qualified semantic-fidelity, native-use, mechanical and operational claims.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":665,
          "quote":"Changed derived guidance, a compression, a frame packet,\na skill or any other projection of the method, is qualified against\nsource-derived expected behaviour, including the affected permissions,\nexclusions and invalidators.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F7.%20Qualification%20of%20frames%20and%20of%20the%20method%2FMethod%20self-qualification",
          "start_line":659
        }
      ],
      "key":"derived_guidance",
      "label":"Derived guidance"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["A selected activation is measured."],
      "definition":"The recoverability benchmark measures whether a fresh actor given only published documents recovers governing relations with independently judged fidelity and at what reconstruction cost, against the rc.2 corpus baseline. It supplies the observable for matrix criteria F27 and F28; F29 and F30 additionally report completed task, detected material errors and cost.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":693,
          "quote":"For a selected activation, measure whether a fresh actor given\nonly the published documents recovers the governing relations with fidelity\njudged independently, and at what reconstruction cost, against the rc.2\ncorpus as baseline.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F7.%20Qualification%20of%20frames%20and%20of%20the%20method%2FMethod%20self-qualification",
          "start_line":690
        }
      ],
      "key":"recoverability_benchmark",
      "label":"Recoverability benchmark"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition":"EVALUATION's source-disposition register records authored conservation and relocation claims concerning rc.2 clauses, received anchors and newly introduced rules. It is trace metadata, not an independent decision or proof of conservation.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":727,
          "quote":"Also new: the tables of families F6 (§1) and F7 (§6), in\nthe format of FOUNDATIONS §3 Foundation to law; each decides nothing and\nnames the rule that states each consequence.",
          "section_id":"repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F8.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line":725
        }
      ],
      "key":"source_disposition_register",
      "label":"Authored source-disposition register"
    },
    {
      "conditions": ["The Product separately adopts the optional profile."],
      "definition":"EXECUTIVE_BASELINE is a tier-3 optional operational profile for one configuration with Executive, Worker and Reviewer engagement frames and specialist families. It owns only its mappings, added terms and choices: direct-entry additions, empirical revision boundary, frame grants and limits, specialist catalogue, Executive procedure, Worker work-result readings, and adoption, project-configuration, qualification and closure records. It imports rather than redefines the underlying method objects.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":15,
